# Pi performance and pipeline first

RTDMS deploys to **one Raspberry Pi 5** running face + phone + physiology + fusion
concurrently. Every design and code choice must optimize for that pipeline, not for
laptop convenience or single-model demos.

## Priority order (highest first)

1. **Concurrent Pi budget** — CPU, RSS, latency, thermals with all models live
2. **End-to-end alert latency** — report target under 500 ms system; phone alert under 1 s
3. **Shared capture / rate control** — one camera reader; decimate heavy models (phone ~5 Hz)
4. **Thread discipline** — `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1`, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, no BLAS oversubscribe
5. **Exportable edge artifacts** — ONNX/INT8 (Hailo HEF later); no training stacks on the Pi
6. **Laptop DX** — useful, but never at the cost of 1–5

## Hard rules for agents

- Prefer a change that is **slower to write but cheaper on the Pi** over a clever laptop-only path.
- Do **not** merge face / phone / physiology into one neural net to “save” processes — optimize I/O
  and scheduling instead (shared frame ring, decimation, separate sockets into fusion).
- Do **not** add per-frame work to a model that fusion only needs at 1–5 Hz.
- Do **not** claim a resource budget from a standalone bench; gate on **concurrent** measurement
  (see `Subsystems/Pi Setup Plan.md`, `models/*/docs/EVIDENCE.md`).
- Before proposing PyTorch/TF runtime on the Pi: require an ONNX (or Hailo) path.
- When unsure between accuracy and Pi headroom: keep accuracy gates for the **graded metric**,
  cut resolution / FPS / model size first (224–320 px, nano weights, 5 Hz phone).

## Pipeline shape to protect

```
capture (1x) → face (~15 fps) + phone (~5 Hz)
BLE/replay → physiology
all three → fusion → GPIO alerts
```

Models **report** (scores + quality). Fusion **decides** alerts. Demo buzzers in model `run.py`
files are not the production path.

